Project: Employee Management System (EMS)
Prepared By: A. Rakesh
Version: 1.0
1. Project Introduction
EMS is a web-based application to manage employee records, attendance, departments and salaries.
2. KT Objective
Transfer complete functional and technical knowledge for maintenance.
3. Business Problem
Manual employee management causes duplication and inconsistency.
4. Project Scope
Covers registration, attendance, departments, reports and admin controls.
5. Target Users
Administrator, HR, Manager, Employee and Support Team.
6. Technology Stack
HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Spring Boot, MySQL, Tomcat and Git.
7. System Architecture
Three-tier architecture: Presentation, Business and Data layer.
8. Application Workflow
Login → Authentication → Dashboard → Database → Response.
9. Login Module
Secure authentication with encrypted passwords and session management.
10. Dashboard Module
Displays employee count, attendance and department statistics.
11. Employee Registration
Stores ID, name, email, phone, salary and department.
12. Employee Search
Search by ID, name, email or department.
13. Employee Update
Modify employee details while preserving data integrity.
14. Employee Deletion
Only administrators can permanently remove records.
15. Department Module
Manages HR, IT, Finance, Marketing and Operations.
16. Attendance Module
Records check-in, check-out and attendance status.
17. Leave Management
Employees apply; managers approve or reject requests.
18. Salary Module
Calculates gross pay, deductions and net salary.
19. Report Generation
Generate employee, attendance and salary reports.
20. Database Tables
Users, Employees, Departments, Attendance, Leave and Salary.
21. ER Relationships
One department has many employees; one employee has many attendance records.
22. API Design
REST endpoints for login and CRUD operations.
23. Folder Structure
Controller, Service, Repository, Model and Config layers.
24. Coding Standards
MVC pattern, camelCase and reusable services.
25. Authentication Flow
Credential validation followed by secure session creation.
26. Authorization
Role-based access for Admin, HR, Manager and Employee.
27. Security Features
SQL injection prevention, hashing and input validation.
28. Exception Handling
Handles login, duplicate records and database errors.
29. Logging
Stores login history, API requests and system errors.
30. Deployment Requirements
Java 17, Maven, MySQL and Tomcat.
31. Deployment Steps
Clone → Configure → Build → Deploy → Verify.
32. Configuration
Datasource URL, username, password and server port.
33. Backup Strategy
Daily incremental and weekly full database backups.
34. Performance Optimization
Indexing, caching, pagination and optimized queries.
35. Testing Strategy
Unit, Integration, System, Regression and UAT.
36. Known Issues
Biometric attendance and mobile optimization pending.
37. Troubleshooting
Restart services, verify DB and rebuild indexes when needed.
38. Future Enhancements
Cloud deployment, AI analytics and mobile app support.
39. KT Handover Checklist
Source code, database, APIs, tests and documentation shared.
40. Conclusion
The KT enables any new developer to understand, deploy and maintain EMS efficiently.
